White light-emitting diodes (WLEDs) are of particular importance in solid-state lighting technology, which has the potential to significantly cut the energy consumption and decrease the greenhouse gas emission.[1] They are generally produced utilizing blue InGaN LEDs coated with a yellow-emitting YAG:Ce phosphor or near-UV InGaN LEDs with a blend of red-, green-, and blue-emitting phosphors.[2] However, these multi-component strategies have problems such as intrinsic color balance, selfadsorption and device complication.[2-5] Thus, single-source white-light emitters are a major target in developing WLED.
Recently, broadband white-light emission as an intrinsic property has been realized in ${<}110>$ -oriented corrugated 2D or 1D organolead halide perovskites, which is attributed to the formation of self-trapped excitons.[6-12] This promising class of white-light emitters is amenable to crystal engineering for further enhancing their photoluminescence properties.[13-18] However, the intrinsic moisture instability of lead perovskites has not been fully addressed.[19]
Luminescent metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are considered to be an ideal platform for single-phase white-light photoemitters, owing to their capability to organize multiple photoemissive components into a single lattice.[20,21] MOFs with open topology offer unique advantages that a vast array of bulky luminescent groups can be attached onto the organic ligands via postsynthetic modification.[22,23] In addition, the permanent porosity of MOFs will bring opportunities to encapsulate luminescent guest molecules.[24,25] The well-defined pore environment along with multiple luminescent centers either on the framework or residing in the porosity enable the MOFs to be a very promising alternative to the conventional white-lightemissive phosphors.
Up-to-date, the vast majority of white-light-emitting MOFs are attained by controlling the relative amount of rare earth (RE) metals with characteristic $_{f-f}$ emissions.[26]  However, the high demands of RE elements have sharply increased the price by up to 49-fold from 2001 to 2011.[27]  Other white-light-emitting MOFs are either ligand-based luminescence or based on metal-toligand/ligand-to-metal charge transfer (MLCT/LMCT) or a combination of both.[28,29] Among them, very few emit broadband white-light emission spanning the entire visible spectrum with high color-rendering index (CRI).[30]
Herein, we describe the incorporation of structurally deformable lead halide units that are often observed in organolead halide perovskites into MOFs. The deformable $[\mathsf{P b}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{3}]^{+}$ $x=0$ , Br, and I) 1-D chains as the secondary building units (SBUs) were interconnected by 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (bdc) as the strut to afford a series of white-light-emitting MOFs $([\mathsf{P b}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{3}^{\bullet}][\mathsf{\bar{O}}_{2}\mathsf{C}(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{4})\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{\bar{\Phi}}]_{2}\bullet[(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{H}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}^{\bullet}]_{3}$ $(X=C l/\mathsf{B r/l})$ , which we denote as TMOF-5(X), TMOF $\dot{\mathbf{\eta}}=\mathbf{\eta}$ Tongji University).  Upon near-UV excitation, the resultant materials are a rare class of isoreticular MOFs with tunable bandgaps that featuring intrinsic broadband emission with high color rendering index (CRI) of up to 89, satisfying the color properties required by WLED.  In addition, the MOF-based photoemitters are air/moisture resistant and provides a versatile platform for further functionalization and tunability.
The solvothermal reactions of $\mathsf{P b C l}_{2}$ , bdc and perchloric acid in DMF/EtOH afford needle-like colorless crystals of TMOF5(Cl).   X-ray crystallography reveals TMOF-5(Cl) consists of doubly interpenetrated nets defined in the tetragonal crystal system, which is due to the tetragonal arrangement of the 1D rod-shaped $[P b_{2}C l_{3}]^{+}$ SBUs in each independent framework (Figure 1a and S1).  The lead halide SBUs comprise asymmetric $\mathsf{P b}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ units in distorted square geometry, which propagates along $c$ axis via $\upmu_{2}{-}\mathsf{C l}$ in a zigzag manner with the $\mathsf{P b-(\upmu_{2})C l-P b}$ bond angles of $132.885(6)^{\circ}$ (Figure 1b). In contrast to conventional 1D metal-oxo SBUs in MIL-53 and MOF-74,[31,32] both of the $\mathsf{P b}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ units and the $\upmu_{2}{-}\mathsf{C l}$ atoms are subject to large lattice rearrangement, that are more likely to introduce structural deformation and broadband photoemission.  The positive charge on the metal halide SBUs is outnumbered by the anionic charge of the ligands, thus rendering the overall anionic $1^{1}0^{2}$ framework. Indeed, the negative charge of the framework is compensated by extraframework protonated dimethylamine cations $((C H_{3})_{2}N H_{2}^{+})$ residing in the 1D pore channel along $c$ axis
(Figure S2).
Importantly, the isoreticular synthesis aimed at isostructural substitution of Cl atoms by other halogen atoms have yielded TMOF-5(Br) and TMOF-5(I) with identical Pb-X $(X{=}\mathsf{B r}/\mathsf{I})$ chains along c axis (Figure 1b).
The substitution of halogen anions introduces anisotropic lattice expansion from TMOF-5(Cl) to TMOF-5(I), which is $\sim6.46\%$ increase of $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ axis but merely $\sim1.49\%$ increase of the other two axes. All three MOFs are attained with high yields and high phase purity, which are evidenced by a good match between experimental powder X-ray diffraction patterns and the theoretical patterns simulated from single-crystal data (Figure S3-S5).  Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of TMOF-5 shows their thermal stability up to $180^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S6-S8). More importantly, this class of MOFs demonstrate high chemical stability in polar organic solutions (e.g. EtOH, DMF) as well as a wide range of relative humidity $(50\sim90\%)$ , overcoming the moisture-sensitive problems of perovskites (Figure S4-S5).
Absorption spectra of TMOF-5 indicate the typical halide-dependent optical bandgaps of 3.38 eV (367 nm) for the chloride analog, $3.20~\mathsf{e V}$ $388~\mathsf{n m})$ for the bromide analog, 2.94 eV $(422~\mathsf{n m})$ ) for the iodide analog, consistent with the trend of organolead halide perovskites (Figure 2a, Figure 2b and Figure S9).  In addition, the predominantly halide-based valence bands are evidenced by density functional theory (DFT) calculations. The total density of states (DOS) of TMOF5(Br) indicate that the valence band maximum (VBM) is dominated by the full Br-4p orbitals (Figure S10).  Meanwhile, the Pb-carboxylate coordination leads to the contribution of bdc ligands to the conduction band minimum (CBM).  The underestimation of the band gap values (calc. 2.50 eV vs. obs. 3.20 eV) is a common result for the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) method.[33]
Upon near-UV $(380~\mathsf{n m})$ excitation, all of the three TMOF-5 materials exhibit large Stokes-shifted broadband photoemissions covering the full range of the visible spectrum $(420{\sim}760\ \mathsf{n m})$ with a maximum emission wavelength at $\sim560$ nm (Figure 2a, Figure 2b and Figure S9). The photoemission of TMOF-5(Cl) and TMOF-5(Br) exhibit extremely large full width at half maximum (FWHM) of 207 nm (0.94 eV) and 205 nm (0.91 eV), respectively.  Importantly, the broad emission from the bromide analog has Commission International de l’Eclairage (CIE) chromaticity coordinates of (0.37, 0.39) that are close to the pure white light of (0.33, 0.33), a correlated color temperature (CCT)
of $4258~\mathsf{K}$ that affords bright white light, and an ultrahigh CRI of 89 (Figure 2d).   To note, CRI is an important parameter of WLEDs to describe how well the photoemission renders the color of an object,[34] and a CRI of 80 is needed for human eyefriendly lighting.  In addition, the photoemission from the chloride analog exhibits an eye-friendly bright white light (CCT of $4784\mathsf{K}$ and CRI of 85) with the highest external photoluminescence quantum yield (PLQY, $6\sim8\%$ ) among our three TMOF-5 materials (Table S2).
Given TMOF-5 presenting as a class of ultrastable broadband photoemitters, we continue to perform photophysics studies and DFT calculations to investigate the orgin of the broadband emission.  First, both cm-sized single crystals and μm-sized microcrystals of TMOF-5 exhibit nearly identical photoemission, ruling out the contribution of surface defects to the photoemission.  The less intense high-energy shoulder at \~470 nm in cm-sized bulk crystals may be attributed to the stronger self-absorption (Figure 2a and Figure 2b).[11]
After evidencing the intrinsic nature of the photoemission from TMOF-5, a likely cause for such large-Stokes shifted broadband emission is the formation of self-trapped excitons from structural deformation in the excited state.[35] Spin-triplet exciton was applied to calculate the excited-state structural deformation of TMOF-5(Br) (see calculation details in ESI), owing to self-trapped excitons in many metal halides are known to have singlet-state to triplet-state transition.[36] It is found that the 1D $[P b_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{3}]^{+}$ chains are the only component exhibiting the structural deformation, in which the $\upmu_{2}$ -Br atoms that bridge the adjacent $\mathsf{P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ square units play an important role (Figure 3d). This finding agrees well with the earlier study by Karunadasa et. al. that only the out-of-plane $\mathsf{P b}$ - $(\upmu$ -Br)-Pb angle (among a total of 52 structural parameters) is closely correlated with the intensity of broadband emission.[37] The zigzag chain is substantially flattened with the $\mathsf{P b-}(\upmu_{2})\mathsf{B r-P b}$ bond angle increase from $128.488^{\circ}$ to $146.892^{\circ}$ (Figure 3c). The large distortion $(D_{\mathrm{tilt}})$ value of $\sim51.5^{\circ}$ is larger than many white-light-emitting perovskites in the previous study.[37] Besides, the $\mathsf{P b}_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ square units become more distorted with the higher tendency to form self-trapped holes $(\mathsf{B r}_{2}\bar{)}$ from Br-Br dimerization.  Both the calculated optical excitation energy (3.42 eV) and emission energy $(2.62~\mathsf{e V})$ correspond well to the experimental values (ex: $3.06~\mathsf{e V}$ ; em: $2.21\ \mathsf{e V}$ , evidencing the large-Stokes shifted broadband emission are well-explained by DFT calculation.
In order to further confirm the role of $\upmu_{2}$ -Br atoms from the $[P b_{2}\mathsf{B r}_{3}]^{+}$ chains to the structural deformation and the broadband emission, we have successfully synthesized another MOF $({\sf P b}_{3}{\sf B r}({\sf b p d c})_{2}$ , bpdc $\scriptstyle:=4,4^{\prime}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate, which we denote as TMOF-6(Br)) consisting of a new bromoplumbate structure motif with substantially decreased structural distortion (Figure 1d).[38]  The resultant 1D infinite tube-shaped $[P b_{3}B\mathsf{r}]^{5+}$ chains are four $\mathsf{P b}$ atom wide and highly symmetrical (Figure S11), in which the $\upmu_{4}$ -Br adopts a typical tetrahedral $\upmu_{4}$ -coordination mode to four $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ atoms with no distortion.  Both high-symmetry $[P b_{3}\mathsf{B r}]^{5+}$ structural motif and the highly coordinated $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ and Br- ions limit the overall structural strain and the distortion of the inorganic SBUs.  Indeed, the photoemission of TMOF-6(Br) only exhibits a narrow UV-blue emission centered at $402\mathsf{n m}$ with a low FWHM of $61~\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 2d and 3d). The high-energy narrow emission of TMOF-6(Br) is probably ascribed to the free excitons with emission decay lifetime of 53.9 ns at $77~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S12), while the broadband emission of TMOF-5(Br) has an average lifetime of $0.53~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ at 77 K, agreeing with the mechanism of self-trapped excitons (Figure S13).
The variable-temperature photoemission measurement of TMOF-5(Br) indicates a more intense and narrower photoemission with the temperature decreasing from $293~\mathsf{K}$ to $153\ K.$ .  This observation agrees with strong electron-phonon coupling in the MOF lattice, which will produce self-trapped excitons in a deformable structure. Assuming the harmonic lattice vibration, the temperature-dependent FWHM is described using the following equation (Figure S14):[6,7,39]
$\Gamma(\mathrm{T})=\stackrel{\smile}{\Gamma_{0}}+\dot{T}_{L O}\bigl(e^{E_{L O}/\check{k}_{B}T}-1\bigr)^{-1}+T_{i n h}e^{-E_{b}/k_{B}T}$ (1) where $\Gamma_{0}$ is the FWHM at $T\ =\ 0\ K,\ E_{\mathrm{LO}}$ represents the longitudinal-optical (LO) phonon energy, $\phantom{+}E_{\mathrm{b}}$ is the average binding energy of trap states.  A best fit to the data indicates the major contribution of electron-phonon coupling $(\Gamma_{\mathsf{L O}}$ of 97(9) meV) to the broadening of the emission and a phonon energy of 13(3) meV that corresponds to the $\mathsf{P b}$ -Br stretching frequency (105 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ from Raman spectroscopy (Figure S15).  Both the phonon energy and the relative contribution of electron-phonon coupling
