of TMOF-5(Br) are close to those of corrugated layered perovskites.[6,7]  In addition, time-resolved photoluminescence decay studies indicate all of the three TMOF-5 materials have photoluminecence lifetime between $1.72\sim4.79$ ns (Figure 3b, S16 and S17), agreeing with the formation of self-trapped excitons from corrugated layered perovskites.[6-10] Overall, the variable-temperature photophysics studies along with the DFT calculations of the excited-state structural deformation as well as the control experiments of TMOF-6(Br) confirm the large-Stokes shifted broadband photoemission from TMOF-5 probably origin from the structural deformation of haloplumbate-based SBUs and the generated self-trapped excitons (Figure 3d).
Incorporation of structural deformable SBUs to MOFs will bring three obvious advantages over organolead halide perovskites, including air/moisture stability, covalent functionalization of photoactive component and high porosity for introducing guest luminophors. First, no significant change in both photoemission intensity and photoemission energy have been observed after irradiating moisture-stable TMOF-5(Br) with UV-light under atmospheric condition $60\%$ relative humidity) for 7 days (Figure S18). Meanwhile, the three white-lightemitting perovskites with higher PLQYs than our MOFs have a significant decrease in emission intensity after 24\~72 h irradiation under the same condition (Figure S19).[7,11,40]  Second, the functionalization of TMOF-5 is achieved by isoreticular synthesis employing 2-amino-terephthalic acid $(N H_{2}$ -bdc), affording amino-functionalized framework that is the typical MOF precursor for postsynthetic modification (Figure 1c).[16]  X-ray crystallography reveals the resultant TMOF-5(Br)- $\cdot N H_{2}$ is isostructural to the parent TMOF-5 with nearly identical unit cells but having the amino-functionalities pointing towards the porosity (Table S3 and S5).  Third, the MOF porosity can be easily used to introduce a second photoemission center, thus further optimizing the overall photoluminescence of the singlephase phosphor.  Considering TMOF-5(Br) occupying an anionic framework with $(C H_{3})_{2}N H_{2}^{+}$ cations residing in the 1D pore channel, cation exchange was performed by incubation of TMOF-5(Br) in a DMF solution containing $1\mathrm{\mol\%}\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ and 5 mol% $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ to afford 0.2 mol%-exchanged and 2 mol%- exchanged $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}@\mathsf{T M O F}{-}5(\mathsf{B r})$ , respectively.  In order to further confirm the exchange process, $\overline{{^1\mathsf{H}}}$ NMR of the digested $2m o l\%$ - exchanged $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}@\mathsf{T M O F}.5(\mathsf{B r})$ confirms the $(C H_{3})_{2}N H_{2}^{+}$ are the outgoing cations (Figure S20).  In addition, no leaching of the $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ species was detected in the supernatant of the exchange mixtures, evidenced by ICP-OES $(<0.1$ ppm).  The $0.2\mathrm{\mol}\%$ - exchanged $E\mathsf{u}^{3+}@$ TMOF-5(Br) exhibits the characteristic $^5\mathsf{D}_{0}..^{7}\mathsf{F}_{\mathsf{J}}$ $\scriptstyle(J=1$ , 2 and 4) transition of $\mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ at $593~\mathsf{n m}$ , $616~\mathsf{n m}$ and $700~\mathsf{n m}$ along with the broadband photoemission from the parent TMOF5 (Figure 4b), thus affording the transition from a CCT of $4258\mathsf{K}$ for bright white-light to a CCT of $7993~\mathsf{K}$ for “cold” white-light (Figure 4c).
In conclusion, we have discovered a class of isoreticular MOFs containing high degree of structural strain of the zigzag $[\mathsf{P b}_{2}\mathsf{X}_{3}]^{+}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{X}=\mathsf{C l}/\mathsf{B r}/\mathsf{I})$ chains.  The resultant MOFs afford largeStokes shifted broadband white-light emitters with FWHMs up to 0.94 eV and CRIs up to 89.   In contrast to densely-packed and moisture-sensitive hybrid inorganic-organic perovskites, our haloplumbate-based MOFs exhibit open framework and undiminished photoemission upon UV excitation in air for over a week.  Moreover, isoreticular functionalization of the organic struts and encapsulation of a second photoemission center have been achieved in our MOFs as well.  Our work significantly advances the excellent charge carrier diffusion properties of organolead halide perovskites into the field of highly porous MOFs, providing a new platform for crystal-engineering of optoelectric MOFs.
